    level: "C1",
    text: "Communication in English extends far beyond simply 'saying' and 'telling'. The language offers dozens of verbs that specify how something is communicated — the volume, emotion, intention, and context behind every utterance. Volume verbs range from whisper (speak very softly) and murmur (speak quietly and indistinctly) to shout (speak loudly), yell (shout with emotion), scream (cry out in fear or excitement), and bellow (shout in a deep, powerful voice). Emotional speech verbs include grumble (complain quietly), moan (express dissatisfaction), sob (speak while crying), stammer and stutter (speak with involuntary repetition), and snap (speak sharply in irritation). Persuasion and argument verbs encompass argue (exchange opposing views), debate (discuss formally), negotiate (discuss to reach agreement), persuade (convince someone), urge (strongly encourage), and insist (demand firmly). Information-sharing verbs distinguish between announce (declare publicly), reveal (make known something hidden), confess (admit something embarrassing or wrong), mention (refer to briefly), and hint (suggest indirectly). Professional communication uses verbs like brief (give concise information), summarise (give the main points), elaborate (explain in more detail), clarify (make clearer), and specify (state precisely). Mastering these verbs transforms reporting and storytelling, allowing speakers to show rather than tell how people communicate.",
